<edge id="R:0bc53907b0" source="C:Method:pseudo-first-order-kinetic-model" target="C:Method:pseudo-second-order-kinetic-model"><data key="e_predicate">COMPARED_WITH</data><data key="e_source_id">paper-B</data><data key="e_strength">direct</data><data key="e_conditions">{&quot;sample&quot;: &quot;rGO&quot;, &quot;criterion&quot;: &quot;R2&quot;}</data><data key="e_paragraph_id">p-0100</data><data key="e_quote">whereas both models provided a comparable fit for rGO</data></edge>
<edge id="R:2cfa5e2574" source="paper-B:Sample:rgo-sample-sigma-aldrich" target="paper-B:Measurement:rgo-kinetics-pfo-r2-0-9973-vs-pso-0-9968"><data key="e_predicate">HAS_RESULT</data><data key="e_source_id">paper-B</data><data key="e_strength">direct</data><data key="e_conditions">{}</data><data key="e_paragraph_id">p-0099</data><data key="e_quote">The pseudo-first-order model exhibited a marginally higher R2 value of 0.9973 compared with 0.9968 for the pseudo-second-order model.</data></edge>
<edge id="R:4f584d488e" source="paper-B:Measurement:hargo-kinetics-pso-r2-0-9939-vs-pfo-0-97" target="paper-B:Condition:kinetics-at-initial-phenol-7-5-mg-dm3"><data key="e_predicate">UNDER_CONDITION</data><data key="e_source_id">paper-B</data><data key="e_strength">direct</data><data key="e_conditions">{}</data><data key="e_paragraph_id">p-0031</data><data key="e_quote">Kinetic experiments were performed at an initial phenol concentration of 7.5 mg/dm3.</data></edge>
<edge id="R:e4b8301c5e" source="paper-B:Measurement:hargo-kinetics-pso-r2-0-9939-vs-pfo-0-97" target="paper-B:Conclusion:pso-pfo-fits-are-empirical-and-do-not-es"><data key="e_predicate">CONTRADICTS</data><data key="e_source_id">paper-B</data><data key="e_strength">direct</data><data key="e_conditions">{}</data><data key="e_paragraph_id">p-0100</data><data key="e_quote">These models should be regarded as empirical descriptions of the adsorption-rate data</data></edge>
<edge id="R:34b3eca17d" source="paper-B:Conclusion:hargo-composite-forms-mainly-through-non" target="C:Mechanism:non-covalent-interactions"><data key="e_predicate">INVOLVES</data><data key="e_source_id">paper-B</data><data key="e_strength">hedged</data><data key="e_conditions">{}</data><data key="e_paragraph_id">p-0081</data><data key="e_quote">This suggests that composite formation occurs mainly through non-covalent interactions between the components.</data></edge>
<edge id="R:b3b6109d60" source="paper-B:Conclusion:hargo-composite-forms-mainly-through-non" target="C:Mechanism:interactions"><data key="e_predicate">INVOLVES</data><data key="e_source_id">paper-B</data><data key="e_strength">hedged</data><data key="e_conditions">{}</data><data key="e_paragraph_id">p-0087</data><data key="e_quote">including π–π stacking of aromatic structures and hydrogen bonding between the functional groups of the components</data></edge>
<edge id="R:e745caf967" source="paper-B:Conclusion:hargo-composite-forms-mainly-through-non" target="C:Mechanism:hydrogen-bonding"><data key="e_predicate">INVOLVES</data><data key="e_source_id">paper-B</data><data key="e_strength">hedged</data><data key="e_conditions">{}</data><data key="e_paragraph_id">p-0087</data><data key="e_quote">including π–π stacking of aromatic structures and hydrogen bonding between the functional groups of the components</data></edge>
<edge id="R:387b947927" source="paper-B:Measurement:langmuir-qm-phenol-hargo-7-10-rgo-6-02-h" target="C:Mechanism:hydrogen-bonding"><data key="e_predicate">ATTRIBUTED_TO</data><data key="e_source_id">paper-B</data><data key="e_strength">hedged</data><data key="e_conditions">{}</data><data key="e_paragraph_id">p-0090</data><data key="e_quote">Carboxyl, phenolic, and hydroxyl groups present in humic acid may participate in hydrogen bonding with phenol molecules</data></edge>
<edge id="R:44944ed1b0" source="paper-B:Measurement:langmuir-qm-phenol-hargo-7-10-rgo-6-02-h" target="C:Mechanism:interactions"><data key="e_predicate">ATTRIBUTED_TO</data><data key="e_source_id">paper-B</data><data key="e_strength">hedged</data><data key="e_conditions">{}</data><data key="e_paragraph_id">p-0090</data><data key="e_quote">the graphene-like aromatic domains of rGO may promote π–π interactions with the aromatic ring of phenol</data></edge>
<edge id="R:6ca7e73ccf" source="paper-B:Conclusion:h-bonding-and-contributions-to-phenol-ad" target="C:Mechanism:interactions"><data key="e_predicate">INVOLVES</data><data key="e_source_id">paper-B</data><data key="e_strength">hedged</data><data key="e_conditions">{}</data><data key="e_paragraph_id">p-0104</data><data key="e_quote">Hydrogen bonding and π–π interactions are therefore considered plausible contributions</data></edge>
<edge id="R:19e8333da3" source="paper-B:Measurement:langmuir-qm-phenol-hargo-7-10-rgo-6-02-h" target="paper-B:Conclusion:results-are-a-preliminary-demonstration"><data key="e_predicate">SUPPORTS</data><data key="e_source_id">paper-B</data><data key="e_strength">direct</data><data key="e_conditions">{}</data><data key="e_paragraph_id">p-0103</data><data key="e_quote">The adsorption capacity of HA–rGO is lower than the values reported for some highly porous graphene-based adsorbents [49].</data></edge>
<edge id="R:313959db79" source="C:Material:reduced-graphene-oxide" target="C:Material:graphene-oxide"><data key="e_predicate">COMPARED_WITH</data><data key="e_source_id">paper-B</data><data key="e_strength">direct</data><data key="e_conditions">{}</data><data key="e_paragraph_id">p-0034</data><data key="e_quote">These results indicate substantial removal of oxygen-containing groups compared with graphene oxide</data></edge>
